// Package server is the Go server half of the contract: a handler interface
// per service, request binding per route, and registration onto any router
// with a Method(method, pattern string, http.Handler) call.
//
// routes_gen.go is generated by cmd/routegen from the same descriptor walk
// that writes the route manifest. runtime.go is hand-written and owns what a
// route-by-route rendering cannot express without repeating itself: the body
// size cap, the seam for verifying raw body octets before they are decoded,
// path-parameter extraction, the error model, and response encoding through
// the contract's MarshalOptions.
//
// # What this package refuses
//
// Persistence, of any kind. Unimplemented<Service> is the whole default
// implementation; wiring a real one to a database, a cache, or another
// service is the implementer's, and nothing here assumes a shape for it.
// Validation beyond what binding requires. Authentication: Runtime.VerifyBody
// is a seam, not a policy.
//
// # What the router owns, not this package
//
// Mux is the registration call and nothing else, so everything a router
// decides before a handler runs is the router's answer, not the contract's.
// Measured against net/http's ServeMux and github.com/go-chi/chi/v5 v5.1.0,
// the two routers internal/chitest exercises:
//
//   - Path-parameter escaping. ServeMux percent-decodes a segment before
//     r.PathValue returns it; chi does not. This one is not left to the
//     router, because the generated TypeScript client percent-encodes every
//     segment and the two halves would then disagree about what an id is.
//     Runtime.PathValue owns it: see StdPathValue and EscapedPathValue.
//   - HEAD on a GET route. ServeMux matches it (Go 1.22+ pattern semantics)
//     and answers 200 with the body suppressed; chi answers 405.
//   - 404 and 405 bodies. Neither is the {"error","code"} envelope: they come
//     from the router before any generated handler runs. ServeMux writes
//     text/plain; chi writes an empty body with an Allow header. A client
//     must not assume a non-2xx response parses as JSON.
//   - Path cleaning. ServeMux redirects "/a//b" to "/a/b" with a 301; chi
//     does not.
//
// # Request handling, in order
//
// For a route declaring body: "*", the body is read once under the cap,
// handed to Runtime.VerifyBody as the exact received octets, then decoded
// with the contract's UnmarshalOptions. Unknown query parameters are then
// rejected on every route, whether or not it declares any. Path parameters
// are bound last and win: a body may repeat a path-bound field, because a
// request message models the whole request, but a value that disagrees with
// the path is a 400.
//
// Request Content-Type is not inspected, and a body sent to a route that
// declares none is never read. Both follow from binding being driven by the
// route's declaration rather than by the request.
//
// # Decisions behind this surface
//
// Mux is one method, Method(method, pattern string, http.Handler), which is
// exactly chi.Router's own. A chi.Router therefore satisfies Mux with no
// adapter and no chi in this module's go.mod; a *http.ServeMux needs StdMux,
// because it has no method-specific registration call. internal/chitest, a
// module of its own so its dependency graph stays out of the published one,
// runs the generated routes against real chi.
//
// Unimplemented<Service> is kept, at a known cost: an implementer who embeds
// it and then renames an rpc still compiles, and the renamed route answers
// 501 at runtime instead of failing the build. The alternative — no
// embedding, so a rename is a compile error — trades that for a service that
// can never be implemented one route at a time, which is the shape every
// consumer of this contract is actually in.
//
// The error model is an ad hoc {"error": "<message>", "code": "<code>"}
// envelope, encoded as a google.protobuf.Struct through the contract's own
// Marshal, so encoding/json stays out of the module. The contract declares no
// error message; whether it should grow one is an open question recorded in
// the repository README rather than answered here.
//
// Runtime.Prefix is the path every route hangs off, and "" means no prefix —
// the shape a router already mounted at the contract's prefix needs. A router
// mounted at the origin root wants Prefix: routes.Prefix. The client mirrors
// this with an optional constructor argument defaulting to the same value.
package server
